experiment_summary:
The contributions statement describes:
- development of the simulation-and-scoring methodology;
- human scoring experiments;
- neuron puzzles with known ground truth;
- ablation-based scoring as an alternative.
The results validating the metric are not in the provided text.

Formal result (Theorem 1). Its assumptions:
- noiseless smooth invertible mixing x=f(s);
- independent sources with segment-modulated exponential-family log-densities, with a single shared modulated statistic q (V=1, q_{i,0}=0);
- TCL feature dimension m=n;
- full column rank of the T×n modulation-difference matrix L;
- optimal TCL classification.
Under these assumptions, TCL features satisfy q(s) = A h(x) + d with A invertible. The Jacobian log-determinant cancels and the pseudoinverse of L yields the result. The V>1 extension is only sketched. The result covers the noiseless, segment-index-as-auxiliary-variable special case.

Adds further design requirements:
- hide explanations at test time;
- assign participants between subjects to avoid learning effects;
- include a non-informative explanation control for engagement effects (the control scored 0.95, 1.02 and 0.94 on the three datasets);
- omit ground-truth labels;
- balance correct and incorrect predictions;
- provide a memory reservoir.

Preliminary dictionary learning on unspecified billion-parameter models found features firing across languages for several concepts:
- 'uniqueness' (English, Spanish, Portuguese, Russian, German);
- 'two';
- 'sad';
- time quantifications;
- whole/entire;
- 'why' questions.
The evidence is anecdotal, with no quantitative evaluation.
